## Proof

Let the (equal) distance between consecutive signals be $d$. Train $i$ has constant speed $v_i$, so the time it needs to traverse any one segment is
$$p_i \;=\; \frac{d}{v_i}.$$
There are $M = N-1$ segments (machines) and $K$ trains (jobs).

### 1. The relative order of the trains never changes

The track is one-way and trains may only queue (park) at signals; overtaking is impossible. If train $A$ starts ahead of train $B$, then $A$ enters and clears each segment before $B$ can enter it (a segment must be free of moving trains before $B$ may use it), so $A$ is always at least as far along as $B$. Hence the processing order on **every** segment is the same as the initial arrangement $\sigma(1),\sigma(2),\dots,\sigma(K)$.

### 2. Flow-shop model

The system is a permutation flow shop with $M$ machines in series, $K$ jobs available at time $0$, where job $i$ has processing time $p_i$ on every machine, and the job order is $\sigma$ on every machine. Let $C(j,m)$ be the time at which the $j$-th train in the order completes segment $m$. Then
$$
C(j,1)=\sum_{i=1}^{j}p_{\sigma(i)},\qquad
C(j,m)=\max\{C(j,m-1),\,C(j-1,m)\}+p_{\sigma(j)}\quad (m\ge 2),
$$
with $C(0,m)=C(j,0)=0$. The total elapsed time (first departure at time $0$ to last arrival) is $C(K,M)$.

### 3. Closed form

Define the prefix sum $S_j=\sum_{i=1}^{j}p_{\sigma(i)}$ and the running maximum $M_j=\max_{i\le j}p_{\sigma(i)}$.

**Claim.** $\;C(j,m)=S_j+(m-1)\,M_j\;$ for all $j,m$.

*Proof by induction.* For $m=1$: $C(j,1)=S_j=S_j+0\cdot M_j$. ✓  
For $j=1$: iterating the recurrence gives $C(1,m)=m\,p_{\sigma(1)}=S_1+(m-1)M_1$. ✓

For $j\ge2,\,m\ge2$, assume the claim for $(j,m-1)$ and $(j-1,m)$:
$$C(j,m-1)=S_j+(m-2)M_j,\qquad C(j-1,m)=S_{j-1}+(m-1)M_{j-1}.$$
Put $q=p_{\sigma(j)}$, so $S_j=S_{j-1}+q$ and $M_j=\max(M_{j-1},q)$.

- **If $q\le M_{j-1}$** (so $M_j=M_{j-1}$):  
  $C(j,m-1)-C(j-1,m)=\bigl[S_{j-1}+q+(m-2)M_{j-1}\bigr]-\bigl[S_{j-1}+(m-1)M_{j-1}\bigr]=q-M_{j-1}\le0$.  
  Thus $\max=C(j-1,m)$, and $C(j,m)=S_{j-1}+(m-1)M_{j-1}+q=S_j+(m-1)M_j$. ✓

- **If $q>M_{j-1}$** (so $M_j=q$):  
  $C(j,m-1)-C(j-1,m)=\bigl[S_{j-1}+q+(m-2)q\bigr]-\bigl[S_{j-1}+(m-1)M_{j-1}\bigr]=(m-1)(q-M_{j-1})>0$.  
  Thus $\max=C(j,m-1)$, and $C(j,m)=S_j+(m-2)q+q=S_j+(m-1)q=S_j+(m-1)M_j$. ✓

This proves the claim.

### 4. Order-independence

The total elapsed time is
$$
C(K,M)=S_K+(M-1)M_K=\sum_{i=1}^{K}p_i+(N-2)\max_{i}p_i
= d\sum_{i=1}^{K}\frac1{v_i}+(N-2)\,d\max_{i}\frac1{v_i}.
$$
Both $\sum_i p_i$ and $\max_i p_i$ depend only on the **set** of speeds $\{v_1,\dots,v_K\}$ and on $N$—not on the order $\sigma$. Therefore the time between the first train's departure from Signal $1$ and the last train's arrival at Signal $N$ is the same regardless of how the trains are arranged. $\blacksquare$

